  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Minnesota has received $1.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government recorded 30,584 awards in the state with an average award size of $63K. Obligations fell 22.5% year over year. FY2026 totals are partial, fiscal year to date, so the $1.9B figure does not represent a full fiscal year. Historical context shows prior fiscal years with higher totals, including $3B in FY24 and $2.5B in FY25, which frames the current year-to-date decline. Agencies and vendors concentrate spending in predictable areas: defense and agriculture are large buyers, and manufacturers and food processors are prominent recipients. This snapshot highlights where federal spending in Minnesota is concentrated and how the current fiscal year compares to recent full-year totals.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who buys the most federal contracts in Minnesota?",
        "body": "Department of Defense leads buying activity in Minnesota with $621M in obligations. Department of Agriculture follows with $328M, and Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $301M. General Services Administration and Department of Health and Human Services round out the next largest buyers with $156M and $137M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries receive the largest federal spending in Minnesota?",
        "body": "NORTHROP GRUMMAN SYSTEMS CORPORATION is the largest vendor, receiving $216M in obligations. MCGOUGH CONSTRUCTION CO., LLC and CUMMINS POWER GENERATION INC. follow with $106M and $85.4M respectively. Ammunition (except Small Arms) Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $216M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $147M and Cheese Manufacturing at $112M."
      }
    ]
  },
